    "lawCode": "Amd §305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7003\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nrequiring the commissioner of education to make recommendations to the\nboard of regents relating to the adoption of instruction in\ninformation relating to blood, bone marrow and organ and tissue\ndonations in senior high schools\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill requires the commissioner of education to make\nrecommendations to the board of regents relating to the adoption of\ninstruction in information relating to blood, bone marrow and organ\nand tissue donations.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Section 305 of the education law is amended by adding a new\nsubdivision 55 that would require the SED to consider if instruction\nin organ and tissue, blood and bone marrow donations in high schools\ncan be beneficial.\n\nSection 2: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNew York has the unfortunate distinction of being 50th in the nation\nin organ and tissue donation. For a myriad number of reasons, the\nnumber of donors in New York is not nearly enough to meet the needs of\npotential recipients. There are more than 10,000 individuals currently\nwaiting for an organ and tissue donation in the State, and\nunfortunately due to the severe shortage of donors, an individual\npasses away every fifteen hours because they could not get a\ncritically needed donation.\n\nBut it is not just organ and tissue donation that New York must\nimprove on. Blood donations and bone marrow donations are two more\nimportant types of donations that can help save and improve the lives\nof others.\n\nIn New York, the current age of consent to register as a bone marrow\nand organ and tissue donor is 18. The age of consent to donate blood\nis 17 (or 16 with parental consent). Therefore, it is critical our\nstudents be fully informed of the numerous life saving benefits of\neach of these types of donations before they reach the age of consent\nso they can make an informed decision on whether to register as a\ndonor or not. Moreover, students that are fully informed can have\nconstructive dialogues with their family and friends about the\nbenefits of being a donor.\n\nIt goes without saying that being a donor is one of the easiest ways\nto have a profound impact on the lives of others. One organ donor has\nthe ability to save up to 8 lives, and impact up to 50 more.\nSimilarly, one pint of blood from one donor has the ability save up to\n3 lives.\n\nNew York must do better to raise the number of donors in the state.\nOne of the most effective ways to do so is to keep asking and keep\ninforming. This bill will require all students to be informed about\norgan and tissue, bone marrow and blood donations and will hopefully\nresult in a greater number of people becoming donors and saving lives.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: New Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis shall take effect immediately.\n",
